<section class="hero"><div class="wrap">
<span class="eyebrow">About us</span>
<h1>Two students, one clinic, a hundred mothers at a time.</h1>
<p class="lead">Heartlines International was founded in 2026 by Jason Ham and Allison Kim, high school students in San Jose, California, to bring practical health support and real encouragement to mothers who are often overlooked.</p>
</div></section>

<section class="alt"><div class="wrap split">
<div>
<h2>Our story</h2>
<p class="sub">It began with a mission trip to the Philippines in February 2026, where co-founders Jason Ham and Allison Kim saw firsthand the work of a free maternity clinic that cares for about 450&ndash;500 mothers a year, many of them young and on their own. Iron-deficiency anaemia is common and serious in pregnancy,<a class="ref" href="#src1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a> yet mothers often skip medication they don't see as essential, and the emotional weight of new motherhood too often goes unaddressed.<a class="ref" href="#src5" aria-label="Source 5">[5]</a></p>
<p class="sub">Together they set out to build something that lasts: health guides in Tagalog and English, checked by the clinic's medical team, handwritten encouragement from student volunteers, and iron tablets the clinic buys locally, delivered cycle after cycle.</p>
</div>
<div class="quoteblock"><p class="role" style="color:var(--dpink);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px">Our approach</p><h3 style="margin-bottom:10px">Clinic-led, not guesswork.</h3><p>The clinic tells us what its mothers need. Its medical team checks every guide before we print, and its licensed staff buy and give the iron locally.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section><div class="wrap">
<h2>Our progress so far</h2>
<div class="track">
<div class="phase-col">
<h3 class="phase-h">How it started</h3>
<ol>
<li><b>Feb 2026</b>Mission trip to the Philippines; our first visit to the clinic.</li>
<li><b>Mar 2026</b>Back home, we started looking for a way to keep helping after the trip.</li>
<li><b>Apr 2026</b>Reached out to clinics and ministries in the Philippines to find a partner.</li>
<li><b>May 2026</b>First call with the clinic's medical team, who told us what their mothers need most.</li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="phase-col">
<h3 class="phase-h">Building it</h3>
<ol>
<li><b>May&ndash;Aug 2026</b>Drafted our materials: the two health guides and the plan for each mother's kit.</li>
<li><b>Aug 2026</b>Incorporated as a California nonprofit public benefit corporation.</li>
<li><b>Aug&ndash;Sep 2026</b>The clinic's medical team reviewed our two health guides.</li>
<li><b>Oct 2026</b>Applied to the IRS for 501(c)(3) status. Opened our bank account. Launched this site.</li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="phase-col current">
<h3 class="phase-h">Where we are now</h3>
<ol>
<li class="now"><b>Now</b>Preparing for our first event and our first cycle of kits.</li>
<li><b>Nov 8, 2026</b>First fundraiser, at Chipotle in San Jose.</li>
<li><b>Fall 2026</b>First cycle: kits for 100 mothers.</li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</div></section>

<section class="alt"><div class="wrap">
<h2>How a cycle works</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li>The clinic's medical team checks our health guides before anything is printed.</li>
<li>Student volunteers write a card for every mother and pack the kits.</li>
<li>We ship the kits and send funds for iron tablets, which the clinic buys locally.</li>
<li>Clinic staff give each mother her kit during prenatal care and tell us what to improve next time.</li>
</ol>
</div></section>

